{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":37164,"items":[{"abstract":"<jats:p>Noisy social text can be transformed into semantic representations, but evaluating the resulting artifacts is difficult when source records, ontology activations, graph resources, conformance reports, and model inputs are considered separately. The evaluation problem is whether their connections remain inspectable as these artifacts are generated and used. This study examines the above continuity using 1614 Reddit posts about artificial intelligence and technology. Ontology activations produce sibling Resource Description Framework (RDF) and numeric representations linked through stable identifiers. Explicit correspondence checks and controlled fault tests examine these connections, while Shapes Constraint Language (SHACL) checks assess conformance to declared graph constraints. Existing verification checks detect 15 of 22 injected continuity faults. The remaining seven are detected only by supplementary diagnostics. Separate human assessment of 120 validation texts evaluates concept presence and local polarity against the source text, distinguishing extraction quality from artifact continuity. Downstream classification provides secondary evidence of modest mean macro-F1 gains in agreement with inherited lexical labels, varying by model across three seeds on one fixed split. The contribution is an evaluation of governed semantic artifact continuity at the post and variable levels, making the connections among these artifacts and their failure conditions clear and inspectable.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/info17100998","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,10]],"date-time":"2026-10-10T03:18:57Z","timestamp":1791602337000},"title":["Evaluating Semantic Artifact Continuity for Noisy Social Text: RDF Materialization, SHACL Conformance, and Model Input Evidence"],"author":[{"given":"Ricardo","family":"Flores-Moyano","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Departamento de Ingenier\u00eda en Ciencias de la Computaci\u00f3n, Universidad San Francisco de Quito, Quito 170901, Ecuador"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-3575-4493","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Felipe","family":"Rosero-Polo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Departamento de Ingenier\u00eda en Ciencias de la Computaci\u00f3n, Universidad San Francisco de Quito, Quito 170901, Ecuador"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jos\u00e9","family":"Vega-S\u00e1nchez","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Departamento de Ingenier\u00eda en Ciencias de la Computaci\u00f3n, Universidad San Francisco de Quito, Quito 170901, Ecuador"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9379-8151","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Maria","family":"Baldeon-Calisto","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Business, Wake Forest University, Winston-Salem, NC 27109, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2078-2489\/17\/10\/998\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/info17100998","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-20","title":["Supplemental Information 20: Training and validation CTC loss curves along with some examples of text extraction of the CRNN model."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-20","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T08:12:34Z","timestamp":1791879154000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Automatic speech recognition (ASR) has been increasingly adopted for generating transcriptions to train text-to-speech (TTS) models, substantially reducing manual annotation costs. However, transcription errors introduced by ASR systems inevitably degrade TTS performance, and existing approaches lack explicit quality awareness, leaving it unclear whether TTS models can learn to distinguish and respond to varying transcription quality. This study investigates whether explicit quality supervision can enable TTS models to develop quality-aware representations and achieve controllable stable synthesis. This study proposes transcription quality labels (TQL), automatically derived from connectionist temporal classification scores. During training, TQL provides explicit quality supervision, enabling the model to associate transcription quality with acoustic characteristics. During inference, setting TQL to high values guides the model toward stable synthesis that faithfully follows the input text. Under controlled conditions isolating the effect of transcription quality, our best TQL-based model achieves 8.4% word error rate compared to 15.9% for the baseline, a 47% relative reduction. While synthesis accuracy gains are significant, naturalness improvements are modest (mean opinion score of 3.66 vs. 3.55), indicating that TQL primarily enhances synthesis stability rather than perceptual quality. Further analysis confirms that synthesis quality varies systematically with TQL values at inference, demonstrating learned quality-conditioned behavior.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1108\/atsip-02-2026-0015","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]],"date-time":"2026-09-03T11:43:22Z","timestamp":1788435802000},"title":["CTC score-based transcription quality annotation for stable text-to-speech synthesis training on noisy transcriptions"],"author":[{"given":"Jingyi","family":"Feng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nagoya University , Nagoya,","place":["Japan"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yusuke","family":"Yasuda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nagoya University Department of Informatics, , Nagoya,","place":["Japan"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tomoki","family":"Toda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nagoya University , Nagoya,","place":["Japan"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.emerald.com\/atsip\/article-pdf\/15\/1\/532\/11820606\/atsip-02-2026-0015en.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"syndication"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.emerald.com\/atsip\/article-pdf\/15\/1\/532\/11820606\/atsip-02-2026-0015en.pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1108\/atsip-02-2026-0015","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7445919","title":["A Rule-Based Text Mining and Information Extraction Framework for Indian Parliamentary Question-Answer Documents,A Pilot Study on Lok Sabha Records"],"author":[{"given":"Sivakumar","family":"S","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Legislative question-answer (Q&amp;A) records published by the Parliament of India on the Sansad and eParlib portals constitute a large, continuously growing, and largely unstructured repository of official government responses covering health, infrastructure, finance, and social welfare policy. Manually locating, comparing, and quantifying information across these documents is time-consuming and error-prone. This paper proposes a five-layer rule-based architecture - Ingestion, Normalization, Extraction, Analysis, and Output - for converting Lok Sabha Starred and Unstarred Question PDF documents into structured, analyzable data. The architecture is demonstrated on a pilot corpus of ten Lok Sabha Q&amp;A documents drawn directly from the Sansad and eParlib portals, spanning eight ministries (Health and Family Welfare, Home Affairs, Women and Child Development, Jal Shakti, Road Transport and Highways, Corporate Affairs, Earth Sciences, and Finance). The pipeline performs automatic metadata parsing, rule-based extraction of government scheme acronyms, detection of embedded state-wise and year-wise statistical tables, and lexical frequency analysis. On this pilot corpus, the system correctly parsed metadata for all ten documents, identified structured numeric tables in five of them, and extracted 15 distinct scheme\/programme acronyms with no manual correction. Two case studies -\u00a0state-wise heatstroke mortality data and the National Family Health Survey (NFHS) child-stunting trend (1992-2024) - illustrate the framework&amp;apos;s ability to recover time-series and cross-state statistics directly from free-text answers. The results indicate that a lightweight, fully auditable rule-based approach can reliably structure short-form government Q&amp;A text without requiring large annotated training sets, and can serve as a preprocessing stage for higher-level classification or retrieval systems.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7445919","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,17]],"date-time":"2026-09-17T15:33:43Z","timestamp":1789659223000}},{"DOI":"10.54254\/2755-2721\/2026.36442","title":["Research on Text Information Extraction and Imbalanced Classification Methods for Enterprise Profiling"],"author":[{"given":"Xinyi","family":"Xu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Tongji University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>This research focuses on enterprise profiling in scenarios where large volumes of diverse texts\u2014such as registration records, annual reports, news articles, and bidding notices\u2014are continuously generated. Instead of relying solely on a single data representation or classification model, we developed a comprehensive natural language processing (NLP) pipeline for extracting key information and identifying industries. The pipeline consists of several steps. First, we use a BERT-BiLSTM-CRF model to identify core e\/nterprise entities. Then, we combine TF-IDF with BERT embeddings to create a hybrid feature scheme that captures both lexical cues and contextual semantics. To address the challenge of imbalanced industry labels, we apply SMOTE in the dense semantic space and pair it with Focal Loss to enhance learning for minority classes. Additionally, we introduce a Stacking strategy to integrate outputs from different models, making predictions more stable. Tests on a self-compiled dataset covering ten national economic sectors and about 50,000 enterprises show that our method achieves a macro-F1 score of 95.4%. It outperforms traditional machine learning baselines and single deep learning models, offering more reliable recognition for minority classes. These results suggest that our framework is well-suited for applications such as supply chain partner discovery, industrial mapping, and targeted investment promotion.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.54254\/2755-2721\/2026.36442","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]],"date-time":"2026-09-01T03:03:32Z","timestamp":1788231812000}},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) achieve strong performance in multimodal information extraction (MIE). In healthcare and elderly-care applications, restricted data access, privacy constraints, and scarce domain-specific annotations often favor inference with frozen models. Standard evaluation measures whether extracted results match reference labels but does not test whether a result changes when a text span identified by the model as its support is modified.\nControlled Text-Anchored Evidence-Dependence Verification for Low-Resource Multimodal Information Extraction (CEDV-MIE) implements this test as a bounded inference-time procedure. A text span reported as support for an initial schema-constrained extraction is modified under validity constraints, while the paired image, visual contextual records, and non-target text remain fixed. The original and perturbed extractions are compared to produce a prediction-level consistency record and a final schema-constrained extraction. No parameter updates or additional evidence annotations are required.\nAcross four public MIE benchmarks and four frozen backbones, CEDV-MIE improves average F\u2081 over direct prompting in all 16 dataset\u2013backbone configurations, with a mean gain of 3.85 points (range: 1.48\u201310.78). Against a self-consistency control using seven chain-of-thought samples per input (SC-7), CEDV-MIE achieves higher average F\u2081 in 14 of 16 configurations under the same maximum allowance of seven backbone calls. The post-hoc Consistency Check Score (CCS) increases in all eight diagnostic configurations evaluated with two backbones (mean +0.195); evidence-stability accuracy and F\u2081 also increase on the validated diagnostic subset.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11232338\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,5]],"date-time":"2026-10-05T08:04:06Z","timestamp":1791187446000},"title":["CEDV-MIE: Controlled Text-Anchored Evidence-Dependence Verification for Low-Resource Multimodal Information Extraction"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0424-629X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaoming","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhongyuan University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yinghui","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhongyuan University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Guan","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhongyuan University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Naveed","family":"Iqbal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King Fahd University of Petroleum and Minerals"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"family":"XuepingPeng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Technology Sydney"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jie","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"North China University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Long","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Durham University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11232338\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11232338\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11232338\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,5]]}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-10","title":["Supplemental Information 10: End-to-end pipeline: from the input image through component and text detection, OCR, node extraction, and component-to-node mapping to netlist generation and circuit solutions."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-10","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T08:12:34Z","timestamp":1791879154000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities in document key\u2013value pair(KVP) extraction, yet controlled evaluations of their robustness to optical character recognition (OCR)output remain limited. This leaves an important gap in understanding their reliability within real-world OCR-to-LLM pipelines. Unlike end-to-end Vision-Language Models (VLMs) that conflate visualperception with semantic extraction, modular pipelines allow errors to be explicitly isolated andaudited. To bridge this evaluation gap, we introduce a controlled, large-scale benchmarking frameworkdesigned to distinguish downstream LLM extraction behavior from upstream OCR degradation. Thisbenchmark evaluates 136 experimental configurations and 17,688 deterministic document-level inferenceinstances across five instruction-tuned open-weight LLMs (2B\u20138B parameters), three complementarydatasets, and four text-quality conditions. This extensive matrix pairs a full zero-shot comparisonand targeted one- to three-shot experiments with a confirmatory sensitivity analysis comprising 40configurations across 20 frozen demonstration sets. By separating Key Recall (annotated-field recovery)from Exact Match and Value F1 (exact and partial value recovery, respectively), we reveal whetherOCR degradation affects field identification and value reproduction differently across models. Crucially,our findings challenge three practical evaluation assumptions: (1) that clean-text performance is areliable proxy for real-world robustness, (2) that model rankings remain consistent across annotation-derived Gold and OCR-derived text conditions, and (3) that providing few-shot demonstrationsmonotonically improves extraction accuracy. By exposing vulnerabilities such as model-rankingreversals and unstable few-shot gains, this benchmark provides a rigorous and reproducible frameworkfor evaluating LLM reliability under noisy document conditions. To facilitate reproducible research,our entire benchmarking framework, dataset splits, and evaluation scripts are open-sourced.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11199953\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,5]],"date-time":"2026-10-05T06:35:06Z","timestamp":1791182106000},"title":["From Pixels to Pairs: A Comprehensive Benchmark of LLM-Driven Key\u2013Value Extraction in Noisy Document Settings"],"author":[{"given":"Zahra","family":"Anvari","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Westcliff University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11199953\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11199953\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11199953\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,5]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7588878","title":["Leverage and Structural Information in Noisy Stochastic-volatility Prices"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-1918-5458","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Joan","family":"Vidal Llaurado","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Leverage can preserve information about a hidden volatility drift as covariance shrinks, provided its response propagates through the latent dynamics. For a bivariate Ornstein\u2013Uhlenbeck model observed through noisy log-price levels on a fixed grid, we prove exact identification at positive covariance and adaptive squared drift risk of order min{1, [n(s\u00b2 + se\u00b2)]\u207b\u00b9}, where n counts observation blocks, s is the covariance scale and e the leverage amplitude. Matching lower bounds on specified drift neighborhoods use the full price law. Dynamically exciting leverage thus permits learning where zero leverage prevents uniform consistency. Nuisance parameters are unknown; confidence coverage includes zero covariance and absent excitation. Precision requires fixed excitation and residual covariance margins, fixed positive level noise, a nonaliased drift branch and a sufficiently small, unevaluated covariance ceiling.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7588878","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,10]],"date-time":"2026-10-10T06:48:47Z","timestamp":1791614927000}},{"DOI":"10.64643\/ijirt.208465-459","title":["Robustness Analysis Of Lightweight Machine Learning Models For Sentiment Classification Under Noisy Text"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64643\/ijirt.208465-459","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,15]],"date-time":"2026-09-15T05:40:16Z","timestamp":1789450816000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/cisat71030.2026.11682145","title":["Reproducible Dual-Path NLP for Character Graph Extraction and Context-Aware State Tracking in Low-Resource Short Text"],"author":[{"given":"Yizhuo","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Qilu Institute of Technology, China","place":["Qufu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/cisat71030.2026.11682145","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,10]],"date-time":"2026-09-10T19:10:05Z","timestamp":1789067405000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11682029\/11682043\/11682145.pdf?arnumber=11682145","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.66977\/xsci.2610.000g","title":["Systematic Review as Recipe Book for Nuanced Emotion Detection in Noisy Text"],"author":[{"given":"Jeff","family":"Winchell","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jamila","family":"Iqbal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nwagu Chima","family":"Ajanwachuku","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Moody","family":"Farra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mostafa","family":"Galal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alonzo","family":"Machiraju","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Recognizing emotion in informal text at the granularity mental health support requires, telling nervousness from fear or remorse from sadness, remains far below usable accuracy: five years of work on Google\u2019s GoEmotions multi-label 28-emotion dataset has reached only .5475 macro F1, and no systematic review has examined why. Accurate emotion recognition is an important predictor of mental healthcare success, yet its subjectivity makes it difficult for even trained human annotators to agree on nuanced emotions in noisy text. Our systematic search identified 494 papers. Inclusion required the full 28-emotion dataset, deep learning models, and reported macro F1, yielding 30 papers and 71 models. Among the highest-scoring models, 9 of the top 10 employ explicit emotion-learning architectures or objective functions. We found no LLM-based models exceeding the foundation paper\u2019s baseline performance. Only 5 of the 71 models underwent extensive hyperparameter tuning. We attributed the observed ceiling to tuning practice as much as architectural limits. Systematic reviews are rare in AI\/ML relative to medicine. This scarcity, combined with a submission surge that has outpaced peer-review capacity, plausibly explains why the field\u2019s true state-of-the-art result went unrecognized for years. This recipe book details the emotion-specific design and tuning techniques associated with the strongest results. The strongest results in the corpus are associated with combining explicit emotion-learning architectures, objective functions, and rigorous tuning, though the reviewed models remain well short of the performance likely needed for practical usability in mental healthcare. Current evidence does not support LLMs as a competitive alternative.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.66977\/xsci.2610.000g","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,4]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,4]],"date-time":"2026-10-04T14:02:13Z","timestamp":1791122533000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3801438.3806612","title":["A Transformer-Based Framework for Russian Text Classification and Information Extraction"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-1447-6734","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dandan","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Heilongjiang University of Technology, Jixi, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4288-1566","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yang","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Heilongjiang University of Technology, Jixi, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-2926-4804","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hongling","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Heilongjiang University of Technology, Jixi, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-2974-7613","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lina","family":"Hou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Heilongjiang University of Technology, Jixi, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-2997-3628","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiuru","family":"Deng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Heilongjiang University of Technology, Jixi, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3801438.3806612","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,24]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T15:20:13Z","timestamp":1788535213000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3801438.3806612","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4106\/supp-1","title":["Supplemental Information 1: Feature Extraction."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4106\/supp-1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T08:15:20Z","timestamp":1791792920000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4106","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Handwritten prescription recognition in low-resource healthcare depends not only on the accuracy of the recognition network but also on the quality of the image presented to that network. This study implemented and evaluated a preprocessing and segmentation pipeline designed to convert noisy, variably illuminated and geometrically inconsistent prescription images into standardised inputs suitable for handwritten-text recognition. The workflow was grounded in a quantitative experimental design using the Doctor's Handwritten Prescription BD dataset, comprising 4,680 segmented pharmaceutical-word images and a 60%\/20%\/20% train-validation-test partition. The pipeline combined grayscale conversion, adaptive thresholding and binarization, Hough-transform-based skew correction, morphological denoising, aspect-ratio-preserving resizing and a final segmentation confirmation stage intended for line localization on unsegmented prescription pages. Segmentation performance was evaluated using Intersection over Union (IoU), with a pre-specified acceptance criterion of 0.90 or higher. The source dissertation states that segmentation success was confirmed at or above this threshold, but it does not separately tabulate an exact IoU point estimate; this article therefore reports the threshold-level result without inventing additional precision. The pipeline is technically important because each stage targets a distinct source of variance that can degrade downstream feature extraction: illumination, background noise, rotation, scale and boundary uncertainty. The study further incorporated contextual augmentation to reduce the mismatch between the Bangladesh-origin source corpus and the intended South Sudanese application environment. The results support the preprocessing and localization design as a necessary front end for the Residual U-Net recognition system, while also showing that external validity remains limited by the absence of a fully local prescription corpus. A clinically credible next step is prospective validation using authentic South Sudanese prescription images, with explicit reporting of IoU distributions, failure cases and computational cost on low-resource hardware.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.68258\/pajps_tox.63833","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,16]],"date-time":"2026-10-16T06:47:08Z","timestamp":1792133228000},"title":["From Noisy Prescription Images to Reliable Text-Line Localization: Preprocessing and Segmentation for Handwritten Medical OCR in South Sudan"],"author":[{"given":"Aguet Elizabeth","family":"Deng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/parj.africa\/pajps_tox\/article\/download\/article-35222\/pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/parj.africa\/pajps_tox\/article\/download\/article-35222\/docx","content-type":"application\/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/parj.africa\/pajps_tox\/article\/download\/article-35222\/cover-image","content-type":"image\/png","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/parj.africa\/pajps_tox\/article\/download\/article-35222\/html","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/parj.africa\/pajps_tox\/article\/download\/article-35222\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.68258\/pajps_tox.63833","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]]}},{"DOI":"10.15407\/spqeo29.03.344","title":["Improving accuracy of differential parameter extraction from noisy measurement data"],"author":[{"given":"D.A.","family":"Kuznietsova","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"V. Lashkaryov Institute of Semiconductor Physics of the National Academy of Sciences of Ukraine, 41 Nauki Avenue, 03028 Kyiv, Ukraine"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>The paper addresses the problem of enhancing the accuracy of differential analysis of integral characteristics of semiconductor structures under conditions of experimental noise. It is shown that standard measurement methods with a linear voltage step lead to significant errors in calculating the dimensionless sensitivity, manifesting themselves as non-physical oscillations that complicate the identification of charge transport mechanisms. A comparative analysis of three data processing methods is conducted: the optimal step method, polynomial approximation (Savitzky\u2013Golay filter), and adaptive filtering based on the Kalman algorithm. It is established that application of the optimal step method based on logarithmic current sampling avoids differentiation artifacts without introducing phase delays characteristic of smoothing methods. The proposed approaches are tested on experimental current-voltage characteristics of OLED structures, confirming their suitability for precise diagnostics of electrophysical parameters of semiconductor devices.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.15407\/spqeo29.03.344","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T06:30:30Z","timestamp":1790663430000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Patient-specific phenotype information extraction identifiers and normalizes phenotype entities and links them with individual patients, supporting rare disease research and genotype-phenotype analysis. However, existing datasets rarely combine full-text biomedical content with detailed annotations of patients, phenotypes, related entities, and their associations. In this work, we present PatientPheX, a corpus for patient-specific phenotype information extraction comprising 200 full-text articles, primarily case reports and case series.  The corpus provides mention annotations and normalization for seven biomedical entity types, together with explicit associations between patients and entities across sentences, paragraphs, and sections. Negated findings and composite phenotype mentions are also annotated. Corpus construction combines automated pre-annotation, large language model (LLM)-assisted verification, and manual review and adjudication. Benchmarks cover entity recognition and normalization and patient-phenotype association extraction using gold-standard and predicted entities. The overall document-level entity F1 was 86.95%, while phenotype F1 was 79.33%. The best relation extraction macro-F1 decreased from 84.14% with gold-standard entities to 54.98% with predicted entities, highlighting the combined challenges of phenotype extraction and patient attribution. PatientPheX provides a resource for developing and evaluating patient-centered information extraction methods and supports the construction of structured patient-level knowledge resources for phenotype-driven biomedical research.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.64898\/2026.10.07.757319","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T08:55:10Z","timestamp":1792054510000},"title":["PatientPheX: A Full-Text Biomedical Corpus for Patient-Specific Phenotype Information Extraction"],"author":[{"given":"Xuanlyu","family":"Zhu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5141-0259","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ling","family":"Luo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhijun","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chentao","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yunxiang","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yang","family":"Zhong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tengxiao","family":"Lv","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Juntao","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chao","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Feng","family":"Tian","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuanyuan","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jian","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hongfei","family":"Lin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/syndication.highwire.org\/content\/doi\/10.64898\/2026.10.07.757319","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64898\/2026.10.07.757319","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]]}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4089\/supp-8","title":["Supplemental Information 8: Image encoder &amp; text encoder."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4089\/supp-8","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,22]],"date-time":"2026-09-22T08:00:22Z","timestamp":1790064022000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4089","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/fig-16","title":["Figure 16: Training and validation CTC loss curves along with some examples of text extraction of the CRNN model."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/fig-16","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T08:12:34Z","timestamp":1791879154000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/taslpro.2026.3739173","title":["T2CycleIE: Treating Hybrid Multi-Hop Table-Text Question Answering as Cycle Information Extraction"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-9976-1614","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shu-Xun","family":"Yang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing Institute of Technology","place":["Beijing, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2473-3746","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhijing","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing Institute of Technology","place":["Beijing, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0320-7520","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Heyan","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing Institute of Technology","place":["Beijing, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6795-2311","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xian-Ling","family":"Mao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing Institute of Technology","place":["Beijing, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/taslpro.2026.3739173","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]],"date-time":"2026-09-30T19:19:53Z","timestamp":1790795993000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/10723155\/11302005\/11716181.pdf?arnumber=11716181","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s11128-026-05342-7","title":["Gate teleportation using noisy gates"],"author":[{"given":"Imama Tul Birrah","family":"Khan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Muhammad","family":"Faryad","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s11128-026-05342-7","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T01:50:57Z","timestamp":1790214657000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s11128-026-05342-7.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/article\/10.1007\/s11128-026-05342-7","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s11128-026-05342-7.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/iciss71466.2026.11715055","title":["Robustness of Sensitive Data Detection Against Indonesian Noisy Text in Shadow AI"],"author":[{"given":"Grace Esther","family":"Simanjuntak","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science Bina Nusantara University","place":["Jakarta, Indonesia"],"department":["Computer Science Department"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kuncahyo","family":"Setyo Nugroho","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bina Nusantara University","place":["Jakarta, Indonesia"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mahmud","family":"Isnan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science Bina Nusantara University","place":["Jakarta, Indonesia"],"department":["Computer Science Department"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Bens","family":"Pardamean","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"BINUS Graduate Program \u2013 Master of Computer Science","place":["Jakarta, Indonesia"],"department":["Computer Science Department"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/iciss71466.2026.11715055","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,2]],"date-time":"2026-10-02T19:05:04Z","timestamp":1790967904000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11715014\/11715016\/11715055.pdf?arnumber=11715055","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4084\/supp-5","title":["Supplemental Information 5: Implements advanced player-interaction feature extraction."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4084\/supp-5","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,14]],"date-time":"2026-09-14T08:15:24Z","timestamp":1789373724000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4084","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.ipm.2026.105204","title":["Label-Corrected Weighted Multi-Similarity Loss for noisy cross-modal retrieval"],"author":[{"given":"Shuni","family":"Jiang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shiyuan","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5313-6134","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhixin","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.ipm.2026.105204","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,2]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T07:37:16Z","timestamp":1790840236000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0306457326005947?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0306457326005947?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/access.2026.3732383\/mm1","title":["Input-Data Information Preservation and Protocol-Specific Error Mitigation in Noisy Fixed-Parameter Quantum Front Ends: A Reproducible Simulation Study_supp1-3732383.pdf"],"author":[{"given":"Hongmin","family":"Jung","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/access.2026.3732383\/mm1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T19:03:39Z","timestamp":1789153419000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.1109\/ACCESS.2026.3732383","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icosdia70683.2026.11690457","title":["Evaluating the Robustness of Multilingual Transformer Models on Low-Resource Language Under Real-World Noisy Text Conditions"],"author":[{"given":"Kevin Maxwell","family":"Andreas","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Bina Nusantara University","place":["Jakarta, Indonesia"],"department":["Computer Science Department"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Keane","family":"Clement","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bina Nusantara University","place":["Jakarta, Indonesia"],"department":["Computer Science Department"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rani","family":"Puspita","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bina Nusantara University","place":["Jakarta, Indonesia"],"department":["Computer Science Department"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icosdia70683.2026.11690457","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,18]],"date-time":"2026-09-18T19:29:06Z","timestamp":1789759746000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11690412\/11689899\/11690457.pdf?arnumber=11690457","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1117\/12.3121516","title":["Machine learning based terbium fluorescence spectra extraction for noisy, resolution compromised document verification systems"],"author":[{"given":"Craig","family":"Stewart","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Asha","family":"Venkatesh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Radhakrishna","family":"Prabhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1117\/12.3121516","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,30]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T16:24:51Z","timestamp":1789143891000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Arabic morphological analysis remains a central challenge for natural language processing (NLP). This challenge is embedded within the complex, rich, and multi-layered morphology of Arabic, where orthographic words encode enclitics, root-and-pattern derivations, and other morphosyntactic features. Another dimension of complexity is the coexistence of different varieties of Arabic, including Modern Standard Arabic (MSA), Classical Arabic (CA), and Dialectal Arabic (DA). In this four-phase empirical investigation of Arabic, a new, standardized CoNLL-U benchmark integrating the Arabella corpus (MSA) and the MASAQ corpus (CA) was evaluated across different settings. This paper aims to build a baseline morphological analyzer by evaluating a wider range of supervised systems, including lexicon-lookup, Conditional Random Fields (CRFs), full fine-tuning of Arabic PLMs, and AraT5 configurations. A parameter-efficient LoRA adaptation of QARiB was implemented, preserving most UPOS performance with minor differences. The results of the comparative evaluation of a variety of models across all settings were reported, and significant results were emphasized. The findings demonstrate severe limitations in current capabilities, particularly that cross-domain transfer for fine-grained language-specific tags (XPOS) remains difficult. Also, models struggle with the asymmetry of cross-register transfer, as moving from Classical to Modern Arabic exposes a lack of coverage for modern named entities and punctuation.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/info17100934","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,22]],"date-time":"2026-09-22T15:28:43Z","timestamp":1790090923000},"title":["A Supervised Morphosyntactic Analyzer for Modern and Classical Arabic Text"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4975-6709","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Majdi","family":"Sawalha","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Artificial Intelligence, King Abdullah II School of Information Technology, The University of Jordan, Amman 11942, Jordan"},{"name":"College of Computing and Intelligent Systems, University of Khorfakkan, Khor Fakkan, Sharjah 18119, United Arab Emirates"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2078-2489\/17\/10\/934\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/info17100934","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,22]]}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4065\/supp-2","title":["Supplemental Information 2: Example of the data extraction applied in the study."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4065\/supp-2","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T08:00:27Z","timestamp":1790236827000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4065","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.ic.2026.105537","title":["A general information extraction framework based on formal languages"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5137-1504","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Markus L.","family":"Schmid","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.ic.2026.105537","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T16:08:42Z","timestamp":1791389322000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0890540126001343?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0890540126001343?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.4208\/jics.v4.n2.p115","title":["A Hilbert Transform N-Dimensional Noisy Phase Unwrapping Algorithm"],"author":[{"family":"Abbas Hasan Kattoush","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Communications and Computer Engineering, Tafila Technical University"},{"name":"Tafila, Jordan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Phase  unwrapping  arises  as  a  key  step  in  several  imaging  technologies,  from  which  we \nemphasize  remote  sensing  applications  like  terrain  analysis  and  generation,  topographic  mapping, \ninterferometric synthetic aperture radar (ISAR). Due to various decorrelation factors, there is much noise in \nphase images. Phase noise degrades the visual quality of images and interferograms, increases the errors of \nresults  derived  from  the  ISAR  interferogram,  and  obstruct  phase  unwrapping.  Hilbert  transform  (HT)  as  a \nphase  unwrapping  techniques  was  recently  proposed,  and  its  behavior  in  presence  of  noise  is  unknown.  In \nthis paper, one-dimensional (1-D) HT phase unwrapping algorithm is proposed to detect and eliminate phase \ndiscontinuities  in  N-dimensional  (N-D)  noisy  phase  arrays.  The  behavior  of  this  method  in  presence  of \ndifferent noise levels is studied and compared with the other approach of phase jump detection. The phase \nnoise  filtration  problem  and  its  effect  on  proposed  algorithm  behavior  are  considered.  Tests  of  method \npresented illustrate the validity and effectiveness of proposed algorithm for difficult problems with noisy and \ndiscontinuous original phases.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4208\/jics.v4.n2.p115","published":{"date-parts":[[2009]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T10:37:26Z","timestamp":1790678246000}},{"DOI":"10.70675\/82a04821za958z4c01z8819z430d27248518","title":["Information Extraction for Integrating Multidisciplinary Articles to Co-construct Coastal Knowledge"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-9247-5745","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Julien","family":"Delaunay","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/04mv1z119","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"La Rochelle"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:title>Extraction d'informations pour l'int\u00e9gration d'articles multidisciplinaires en vue de la co-construction de connaissances sur les zones c\u00f4ti\u00e8res<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p xml:lang=\"fr\">Les zones c\u00f4ti\u00e8res, parmi les syst\u00e8mes les plus dynamiques et complexes sur Terre, font face \u00e0 des pressions environnementales croissantes en raison des activit\u00e9s anthropiques et du changement global. La croissance des activit\u00e9s c\u00f4ti\u00e8res a g\u00e9n\u00e9r\u00e9 des int\u00e9r\u00eats concurrents et une fragmentation dans le d\u00e9veloppement c\u00f4tier, conduisant \u00e0 des conflits d'utilisation des terres et \u00e0 une comp\u00e9tition pour les ressources naturelles. Le d\u00e9veloppement rapide des r\u00e9gions c\u00f4ti\u00e8res a eu des cons\u00e9quences environnementales substantielles, incluant des perturbations \u00e9cologiques, de la pollution et une vuln\u00e9rabilit\u00e9 accrue aux al\u00e9as naturels. L'expansion du tourisme et de l'urbanisation a encore exacerb\u00e9 ces d\u00e9fis, affectant de mani\u00e8re disproportionn\u00e9e les communaut\u00e9s pauvres et vuln\u00e9rables. Atteindre une compr\u00e9hension compl\u00e8te du fonctionnement g\u00e9n\u00e9ral du syst\u00e8me n\u00e9cessite une int\u00e9gration approfondie des connaissances interdisciplinaires. Cependant, les connexions interdisciplinaires sont complexes en raison de l'incompatibilit\u00e9 des entr\u00e9es et des sorties \u00e0 travers diff\u00e9rentes disciplines. Cette th\u00e8se pose les bases d'un syst\u00e8me con\u00e7u pour construire automatiquement une base de connaissances interdisciplinaire (KB) ax\u00e9e sur les r\u00f4les de diverses entit\u00e9s au sein des syst\u00e8mes littoraux en utilisant le Deep Learning. Les contributions cl\u00e9s incluent l'analyse de l'\u00e9volution de la recherche c\u00f4ti\u00e8re de 1970 \u00e0 2023, l'\u00e9tude de l'application du cadre ARDI (Acteur, Ressource, Dynamique, Interaction) pour guider la strat\u00e9gie d'annotation, l'emploi de l'extraction automatique de termes (ATE) et de l'extraction de relations au niveau du document (DocRE) pour identifier les termes cl\u00e9s et extraire simultan\u00e9ment les entit\u00e9s et leurs relations \u00e0 partir du jeu de donn\u00e9es. Le manuscrit est organis\u00e9 en sept chapitres, \u00e9tablissant le cadre contextuel et les motivations sous-jacentes \u00e0 la th\u00e8se, fournissant une revue compl\u00e8te de l'\u00e9tat de l'art, pr\u00e9sentant une analyse scientom\u00e9trique de la production de recherche c\u00f4ti\u00e8re, d\u00e9limitant le processus d'annotation du jeu de donn\u00e9es, d\u00e9crivant la m\u00e9thodologie et les r\u00e9sultats de l'ATE, et introduisant le mod\u00e8le pour l'extraction simultan\u00e9e des entit\u00e9s et de leurs relations. La th\u00e8se conclut avec une discussion des r\u00e9sultats et de leurs implications pour la recherche future et la gestion c\u00f4ti\u00e8re.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.70675\/82a04821za958z4c01z8819z430d27248518","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,2]],"date-time":"2026-09-02T15:40:08Z","timestamp":1788363608000}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114\/supp-3","title":["Supplemental Information 3: Summary of the full-text eligibility assessment stage and coded exclusion reasons."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114\/supp-3","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T08:45:22Z","timestamp":1791794722000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Stylometric features and frozen sentence-transformer embeddings are widely used as low-cost inputs for misinformation-related text classification, but heterogeneous benchmark tasks are rarely compared under a common protocol. We evaluated term frequency\u2013inverse document frequency (TF-IDF), a 22-feature stylometric battery, frozen sentence-BERT, and fine-tuned RoBERTa on 15 English-language benchmarks (n = 24,906), including five human\u2013AI provenance corpora. A descriptive partition of the raw area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) values attributed 71.8% of the total sum of squares to task category means and 13.7% to benchmarks within categories; an exact benchmark label permutation yielded p = 0.000161, but category remained confounded with benchmark construction, source, domain, label mapping, and sampling. After correcting three label orientations, the mean off-diagonal transfer AUC was 0.492 for stylometry and 0.507 for sentence-BERT. Retained-character matching reduced the length-only AUC to approximately 0.50, while low-cost methods retained AUCs of 0.779\u20130.999 in four estimable human\u2013AI benchmarks; AITextPile lacked common support. Alternative LIAR\/LIAR2 mappings changed individual estimates and method ordering. Random intake sensitivity was method-dependent: the MAGE length-only AUC fell from 0.936 to a median of 0.476 and AITextPile from 1.000 to 0.560. The results support benchmark- and target-domain-specific validation, not a universal misinformation or AI-text detector.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/info17090866","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]],"date-time":"2026-09-08T09:02:24Z","timestamp":1788858144000},"title":["Benchmark-Task Heterogeneity in Misinformation-Related and Human\u2013AI Text Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Grzegorz","family":"\u015awierk","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Humanities, AGH University of Krakow, ul. Czarnowiejska 36, 30-054 Krak\u00f3w, Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"software","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"visualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"formal-analysis","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-original-draft","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"investigation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"data-curation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4494-3890","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rafa\u0142","family":"Olszowski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Humanities, AGH University of Krakow, ul. Czarnowiejska 36, 30-054 Krak\u00f3w, Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"supervision","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2078-2489\/17\/9\/866\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/info17090866","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tit.2026.3741662","title":["The Fixed-\n                    <i>k<\/i>\n                    Quantization Problem for Noisy Binary Sources"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7305-9968","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Taleb","family":"Moazzeni","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Global College of Engineering and Technology","place":["Muscat, Oman"],"department":["Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tit.2026.3741662","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T19:07:40Z","timestamp":1791486460000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/18\/4667673\/11727094.pdf?arnumber=11727094","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-031-87993-7_675-1","title":["Environmental Law and Terminology: Knowledge Extraction and Representation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7264-4580","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Arianne","family":"Reimerink","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-031-87993-7_675-1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,17]],"date-time":"2026-09-17T02:12:57Z","timestamp":1789611177000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/978-3-031-87993-7_675-1","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114\/supp-8","title":["Supplemental Information 8: Consolidated extraction matrix for the studies included in the final review."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114\/supp-8","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T08:45:22Z","timestamp":1791794722000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.70675\/b446e74fz158dz4bb3z88f7z71c5cf708e55","title":["From Document to Decision : Neurosymbolic Approaches for Information Extraction from Transactional Documents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-7550-2568","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Arthur","family":"Hemmer","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/04mv1z119","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"La Rochelle"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:title>Du document \u00e0 la d\u00e9cision : approches neurosymboliques pour l\u2019extraction d\u2019informations \u00e0 partir de documents transactionnels<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p xml:lang=\"fr\">Cette th\u00e8se porte sur l\u2019am\u00e9lioration de l\u2019extraction d\u2019informations \u00e0 partir de documents en corrigeant les erreurs gr\u00e2ce \u00e0 l\u2019utilisation de connaissances pr\u00e9alables sp\u00e9cifiques au domaine. Elle se concentre sur les documents transactionnels tels que les factures, re\u00e7us et devis, pour lesquels nous formulons des contraintes symboliques permettant de v\u00e9rifier l\u2019exactitude des extractions. Ce m\u00e9canisme de validation sert alors de signal pour explorer des hypoth\u00e8ses d\u2019extraction alternatives jusqu\u2019\u00e0 en identifier une valide. Les hypoth\u00e8ses alternatives proviennent \u00e0 la fois de la reconnaissance optique de caract\u00e8res et de l\u2019\u00e9tape d\u2019extraction d\u2019informations dans la cha\u00eene de traitement documentaire. La validation est \u00e9galement appliqu\u00e9e aux mod\u00e8les de bout en bout via la g\u00e9n\u00e9ration de donn\u00e9es synth\u00e9tiques, o\u00f9 les exemples invalides sont filtr\u00e9s et les exemples restants, de haute qualit\u00e9, sont utilis\u00e9s pour affiner les mod\u00e8les. Les r\u00e9sultats montrent que les approches neurosymboliques permettent d\u2019obtenir une meilleure pr\u00e9cision, en particulier dans des contextes frugaux, la qualit\u00e9 des contraintes jouant alors un r\u00f4le d\u00e9terminant.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.70675\/b446e74fz158dz4bb3z88f7z71c5cf708e55","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T12:57:46Z","timestamp":1788526666000}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-19","title":["Supplemental Information 19: Training and validation curves of the YOLOv8-small model for text region detection."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-19","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T08:12:34Z","timestamp":1791879154000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>The object of the study is the process of lossy compression of noisy grayscale images by modern coders based on discrete cosine transform using several quality metrics. The subject of the study is the method to determine parameters that control compression to provide the highest quality of compressed images or the best compression ratio for the considered type of images.The goal of the study is to analyze performance characteristics for test images of different complexity and produce recommendations concerning the coder selection and its parameters setting. Methods used: numerical simulation, verification for a set of test images. Results obtained: 1) optimal operation point exists in many cases where its existence is more probable for a larger noise intensity, simpler structure images, and more efficient coders; 2) the better portable graphics coder usually provides the best compromise between image quality and compression ratio; 3) a rather high correlation of performance characteristics of compression techniques based on discrete cosine transform is observed. Conclusions: 1) it is often possible to provide a rather large compression ratio without losing valuable information or even with improving the image quality; 2) performance characteristics of lossy compression considerably depends on image complexity and, thus, the parameter that controls compression has to be adapted to image and noise characteristics.\u00a0<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.20998\/2522-9052.2026.4.05","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,2]],"date-time":"2026-10-02T11:39:52Z","timestamp":1790941192000},"title":["PERFORMANCE COMPARISON OF MODERN LOSSY COMPRESSION TECHNIQUES WITH APPLICATION TO GRAYSCALE NOISY IMAGES"],"author":[{"given":"Sergii","family":"Kryvenko","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vladimir","family":"Lukin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Benoit","family":"Vozel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/ais.khpi.edu.ua\/article\/download\/372714\/356274","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/ais.khpi.edu.ua\/article\/download\/372714\/356274","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20998\/2522-9052.2026.4.05","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,2]]}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/fig-6","title":["Figure 6: End-to-end pipeline from the input image through component, text detection, OCR, node extraction, and component-to-node mapping to netlist generation and circuit solutions."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/fig-6","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T08:12:34Z","timestamp":1791879154000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.26512\/2022.05.d.44406","title":["Neural information extraction pipeline for cyber forensics with pre-trained language models"],"author":[{"given":"Fillipe Barros","family":"Rodrigues","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26512\/2022.05.d.44406","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,20]],"date-time":"2026-09-20T20:03:22Z","timestamp":1789934602000}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.inffus.2026.104775","title":["Robust hybrid graph fusion for multi-modal relation extraction via information bottleneck denoising"],"author":[{"given":"Qihui","family":"Zhao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Haoran","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tianhan","family":"Gao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.inffus.2026.104775","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,3]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T09:51:40Z","timestamp":1789120300000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S1566253526006512?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S1566253526006512?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.67599\/anr-23-ce46-0006","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.67599\/anr-23-ce46-0006","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,2]],"date-time":"2026-09-02T15:11:00Z","timestamp":1788361860000}},{"DOI":"10.61686\/gxapp47132","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.61686\/gxapp47132","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T15:37:15Z","timestamp":1788536235000}},{"DOI":"10.67599\/anr-20-iadj-0005","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.67599\/anr-20-iadj-0005","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]],"date-time":"2026-09-03T10:16:48Z","timestamp":1788430608000}},{"DOI":"10.37391\/ijeer.140318","title":["Error Signal Based Method for Real Time Pitch Extraction from Noisy Speech"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-3305-6627","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohammad Forhad","family":"Hossain","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of ICT, Comilla University, Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-7469-2057","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nargis","family":"Parvin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Associate Professor, Department of CSE, Bangladesh Army International University of Science and Technology, Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-0629-486X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Md. Saifur","family":"Rahman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Professor, Department of ICT, Comilla University, Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-6748-2038","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Pintu Chandra","family":"Paul","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Assistant Professor, Department of ICT, Comilla University, Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0077-6093","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Moinur","family":"Rahman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Lecturer, Department of ICT, Comilla University, Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-9066-7720","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Md. Ohidul","family":"Islam","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Assistant Professor, Department of Computer Science & Engineering, Atish Dipankar University of Science and Technology(ADUST), Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Pitch estimation efficiency always gets affected by two major phenomena. The first one is additive noise which by nature are not correlated with the source speech signal. The second one is the vocal tract effect which creates unnecessary peaks within the signal waveform, resulting inaccurate detection of the pitch value. This paper proposes a Modified Weighted Autocorrelation Function (MWAF) executed in the Linear Predictive Coding (LPC) residual domain, instead of directly executing over the noisy source speech signal. Converting the noisy source speech signal to LP residual helps eliminating the vocal tract effects and emphasizing the necessary effects of glottal excitation. The Autocorrelation Function (ACF) and the Circular Average Magnitude Difference Function (CAMDF) are directly applied on this residual. The proposed MWAF is then calculated by multiplying the ACF with the reciprocal of the CAMDF, which enhances the periodic components as well as reduces the noise induced false peaks. Experimental results were evaluated on the KEELE and NTT databases across White, Babble, Train and HF Channel noise conditions. The result shows that our proposed algorithm reduces the Gross Pitch Error (GPE) by an average of 40.34% compared to baseline methods such as BaNa, PEFAC and WAF. Furthermore, the algorithm achieves an overall computational gain of 53.44% compared to BaNa, PEFAC and WAF, offering a stable solution for pitch tracking in degraded acoustic conditions.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.37391\/ijeer.140318","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,9]],"date-time":"2026-10-09T11:52:31Z","timestamp":1791546751000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Reliable attribution of artificial intelligence (AI)-generated text to a specific large language model (LLM) matters increasingly as LLMs proliferate, yet where quantum machine learning actually stands on this task has, to our knowledge, never been measured systematically. We benchmark the quantum support vector machine (QSVM) for binary attribution between Gemma 3 and Qwen 2.5 on a 5800-sample corpus from paired prompts: 83 configurations sweeping qubit count, regularization, training-set size, feature-map family, and circuit depth under exact, noiseless classical statevector simulation. QSVM validation accuracy plateaus at approximately 88%, whereas a classical support vector machine with a radial basis function kernel reaches approximately 97.8% on the identical fourteen-dimensional inputs: the ceiling belongs to the quantum (fidelity) kernel, not to the input representation. We measure the mechanism: off-diagonal quantum kernel values shrink exponentially with qubit count, the signature of exponential kernel concentration. The same classical model recovers the stylometric attribution fingerprint, showing it belongs to the shared feature pipeline rather than to the quantum kernel. All large-scale headline results generalize to an independent 1000-text test set produced after every design decision was frozen. The study provides a cautionary, reproducible benchmark for quantum kernel natural language processing and outlines an open-set extension as future work.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/info17090883","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T12:24:33Z","timestamp":1789129473000},"title":["A Systematic Benchmark of Quantum Support Vector Machines for Interpretable Attribution of AI-Generated Text"],"author":[{"given":"Kalin","family":"Kopanov","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Information and Communication Technologies, Bulgarian Academy of Sciences, Acad. G. Bonchev St., Bl. 2, 1113 Sofia, Bulgaria"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"software","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"formal-analysis","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"investigation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"data-curation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-original-draft","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"visualization","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6084-3179","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tatiana","family":"Atanasova","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Information and Communication Technologies, Bulgarian Academy of Sciences, Acad. G. Bonchev St., Bl. 2, 1113 Sofia, Bulgaria"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"supervision","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"project-administration","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"funding-acquisition","vocabulary":"credit"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2078-2489\/17\/9\/883\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/info17090883","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>The work is devoted to the development of the hardware-software prototype of a voice navigation subsystem intended for assistive navigation under acoustic interference. The relevance of the topic is explained by the need to improve the accuracy of generating voice or haptic prompts regarding the recommended route for visually impaired users based on their voice-based destination requests. The accuracy of the recommended route depends on the accuracy of voice command recognition in noisy and crowded environments. The goal of this paper is to develop a hardware-software module for voice navigation within an assistive system that enables user positioning and command recognition in the presence of acoustic interference, with the goal of enhancing the independence and mobility of individuals with visual and\/or musculoskeletal impairments. The subject of this study is the processes and methods for improving the accuracy and robustness of voice command recognition in a hardware-software navigation subsystem in the presence of stationary and non-stationary noise and a multi-voice acoustic environment. The task of this work is to develop a functional model of the voice navigation hardware-software module, to develop a comprehensive sound processing algorithm that accounts for various types of acoustic interference and voice overlap; to analyze the impact of various types of acoustic interference and voice overlap on the performance of the software-hardware system. Methods such as comparative analysis, experimental models, acoustic signal recognition were applied. The results demonstrate that the proposed preprocessing and speaker-oriented filtering improve speech command recognition quality in all considered cases. WER decreased from 15.4% to 13.2% in reference conditions, from 48.6% to 30.9% under non-stationary noise, from 68.7% to 15.4% under 50 dB stationary noise, and from 73.0% to 35.3% for overlapping speech of equal loudness. The obtained results confirm the practical suitability of the proposed subsystem for assistive navigation systems operating in acoustically challenging real-world environments. Further research will focus on integrating intelligent personalization techniques into navigation feedback.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.20998\/2522-9052.2026.4.03","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,2]],"date-time":"2026-10-02T11:39:52Z","timestamp":1790941192000},"title":["HARDWARE-SOFTWARE PROTOTYPE OF A VOICE NAVIGATION SUBSYSTEM IN NOISY ENVIRONMENTS"],"author":[{"given":"Olesia","family":"Barkovska","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Denys","family":"Storchai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Andriy","family":"Kovalenko","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuri","family":"Romanenkov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vadym","family":"Trunov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/ais.khpi.edu.ua\/article\/download\/372684\/356185","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/ais.khpi.edu.ua\/article\/download\/372684\/356185","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20998\/2522-9052.2026.4.03","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,2]]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/access.2026.3732383","title":["Input-Data Information Preservation and Protocol-Specific Error Mitigation in Noisy Fixed-Parameter Quantum Front Ends: A Reproducible Simulation Study"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-0596-3933","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hongmin","family":"Jung","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Laurel Springs School","place":["Ojai, CA, USA"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/access.2026.3732383","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,10]],"date-time":"2026-09-10T19:03:01Z","timestamp":1789066981000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/6287639\/11323511\/11685949.pdf?arnumber=11685949","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.69894\/20141107","title":["Beyond Recognition offers terabyte text trial"],"author":[{"given":"Neil","family":"McNaughton","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p xml:lang=\"en\">Guided \u2018visual classification\u2019 methodology for document collection rationalization.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.69894\/20141107","published":{"date-parts":[[2014,12,28]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]],"date-time":"2026-09-03T12:14:39Z","timestamp":1788437679000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/re68928.2026.00048","title":["Evaluating the Impact of Input Text Properties on LLM-Based Requirements Extraction"],"author":[{"given":"Konstantin","family":"Valeev","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Utrecht University","place":["Utrecht, The Netherlands"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fabiano","family":"Dalpiaz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Utrecht University","place":["Utrecht, The Netherlands"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fatma Ba\u015fak","family":"Aydemir","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Utrecht University","place":["Utrecht, The Netherlands"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/re68928.2026.00048","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T19:10:24Z","timestamp":1788981024000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11676474\/11676607\/11676732.pdf?arnumber=11676732","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-18","title":["Supplemental Information 18: YOLOv8 nano, small, and medium models\u2019 best parameter evaluation for the text-region localization task."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-18","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T08:12:34Z","timestamp":1791879154000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.47788\/lrbu5139","title":["6. More-Than-Human Reader, Text, and Stone in Samuel Beckett\u2019s Ill Seen Ill Said"],"author":[{"given":"Nilanjana","family":"Bhattacharya","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.47788\/lrbu5139","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,11,19]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T06:45:00Z","timestamp":1790232300000}},{"DOI":"10.69894\/20050130","title":["Open Text Livelink for Vintage Petroleum"],"author":[{"given":"Neil","family":"McNaughton","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p xml:lang=\"en\">Livelink deployed as business process improvement platform. Open Text holds  energy user meet.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.69894\/20050130","published":{"date-parts":[[2005,1,28]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]],"date-time":"2026-09-03T12:20:56Z","timestamp":1788438056000}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7493274","title":["Decomposing the schema: reliable structured information extraction with large language models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8105-4101","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Abdalrhman","family":"Alquaary","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7489-9053","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Numan","family":"\u00c7elebi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Information extraction, converting unstructured text into structured data, has been reshaped by large language models: instead of labelling spans with a fixed type inventory, a single model now populates an entire predefined schema. We study this modern form, structured information extraction, among the most heavily deployed uses of these models. The default one-shot method puts the whole schema and document in one prompt and requests the entire object at once; it is fundamentally fragile, degrading silently or catastrophically as the schema grows denser, the document longer, or the model weaker. We instead decompose the schema itself into independently extractable units, query each unit separately against the document, and reassemble the outputs with a deterministic, schema-validated composer. A unit is a single field when fields are independent; when a list\u2019s records are interdependent and unbounded in number, the entire list is one unit. Reliability matters most where it is hardest to achieve: privacy rules forbid sending the most sensitive documents to hosted frontier models, forcing extraction onto local open-weight models, precisely the weaker models on which one-shot most often fails. We evaluate on a purpose-built, fully released benchmark (800 documents across legal and medical schemas in English and Arabic, constructed ground-truth-first) over 30 open-weight models spanning four families (Qwen, Llama, Gemma, gpt-oss) and three architecture types (dense, Mixture-of-Experts, Matformer). Decomposition lifts value-accuracy macro-F1 over one-shot on every model and recovers cases where one-shot collapses entirely, including models that produce no usable output on most documents.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7493274","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,20]],"date-time":"2026-09-20T13:39:38Z","timestamp":1789911578000}},{"DOI":"10.69894\/20070115","title":["MetaCarta geo-text search engine for SPE"],"author":[{"given":"Neil","family":"McNaughton","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p xml:lang=\"en\">The Society of Petroleum Engineers is to offer geographical, taxonomy-based search of e-Library.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.69894\/20070115","published":{"date-parts":[[2007,1,28]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]],"date-time":"2026-09-03T12:20:21Z","timestamp":1788438021000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/cisat71030.2026.11682402","title":["Robustness Evaluation and Denoising Optimization of Deep Learning Models for Brain Tumor MRI Classification Under Noisy Conditions"],"author":[{"given":"Haowen","family":"Xu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Hainan University","place":["Haikou, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/cisat71030.2026.11682402","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,10]],"date-time":"2026-09-10T19:10:46Z","timestamp":1789067446000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11682029\/11682043\/11682402.pdf?arnumber=11682402","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3805696.3846498","title":["Robust Cross-Modal Alignment for Reliable Multimodal Video Information Access under Noisy Textual Descriptions"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2940-0043","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chengxi","family":"Chu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Universiti Malaya, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-4878-4134","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yi-Shuai","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Universiti Malaya, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3805696.3846498","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T07:02:51Z","timestamp":1791788571000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3805696.3846498","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tcsvt.2026.3732018","title":["Progressive Robust Decoupling of Noisy-Hard Sample for Text-to-Image Person Retrieval"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-9049-1833","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Runqing","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Electronic Science and Technology of China (UESTC)","place":["Chengdu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jin","family":"Ding","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Electronic Science and Technology of China (UESTC)","place":["Chengdu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1654-2157","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xue","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"UESTC","place":["Shenzhen, China"],"department":["Shenzhen Institute for Advanced Study"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mingzhu","family":"Cai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Electronic Science and Technology of China (UESTC)","place":["Chengdu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-4962-8905","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Li","family":"Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Electronic Science and Technology of China (UESTC)","place":["Chengdu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8676-8322","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jianxiao","family":"Zou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"UESTC","place":["Shenzhen, China"],"department":["Shenzhen Institute for Advanced Study"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3023-1662","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xi","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhejiang University (ZJU)","place":["Hangzhou, China"],"department":["College of Computer Science and Technology"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tcsvt.2026.3732018","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T19:02:54Z","timestamp":1788980574000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/76\/4358651\/11684849.pdf?arnumber=11684849","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.32890\/jict2013.12.8","title":["Normalization of Noisy Texts in Malaysian Online Reviews"],"author":[{"given":"Norlela","family":"Samsudin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Computer and Mathematical Science, Universiti Teknologi MARA Terengganu, Dungun, 23000, Terengganu"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mazidah","family":"Puteh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Computer and Mathematical Science, Universiti Teknologi MARA Terengganu, Dungun, 23000, Terengganu"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Abdul Razak","family":"Hamdan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Information Science and Technology, Universiti Kebangsaan Malaysia 43600, Bangi, Selangor"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mohd Zakree","family":"Ahmad Nazri","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Information Science and Technology, Universiti Kebangsaan Malaysia 43600, Bangi, Selangor"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>The process of gathering useful information from online messages has increased as more and more people use the Internet and other online applications such as Facebook and Twitter to communicate with each other. One of the problems in processing online messages is the high number of noisy texts that exist in these messages. Few studies have shown that the noisy texts decreased the result of text mining activities. On the other hand, very few works have investigated on the patterns of noisy texts that are created by Malaysians. In this study, a common noisy terms list and an artificial abbreviations list were created using specific rules and were utilized to select candidates of correct words for a noisy term. Later, the correct term was selected based on a bi-gram words index. The experiments used online messages that were created by the Malaysians. The result shows that normalization of noisy texts using artificial abbreviations list compliments the use of common noisy texts list.\r\n\u00a0<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32890\/jict2013.12.8","published":{"date-parts":[[2013,4,23]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T05:39:07Z","timestamp":1788500347000}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114\/table-11","title":["Table D1: Structured data extraction framework used to code bibliographic, methodological, and empirical information from the included studies."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114\/table-11","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T08:45:22Z","timestamp":1791794722000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-6","title":["Supplemental Information 6: Dataset preparation workflow showing preprocessing, component annotation (six classes), text region annotation, and cropped OCR dataset creation."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-6","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T08:12:34Z","timestamp":1791879154000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.69894\/20051120","title":["Sasol to deploy Open Text for SOX compliance"],"author":[{"given":"Neil","family":"McNaughton","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p xml:lang=\"en\">Internal Controls Solution targets Sarb-Ox reporting, integrates with LiveLink  content management.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.69894\/20051120","published":{"date-parts":[[2005,12,28]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]],"date-time":"2026-09-03T12:20:56Z","timestamp":1788438056000}},{"DOI":"10.48009\/2_iis_2026_115","title":["ANALYZING EMERGING TRENDS IN GEOSPATIAL ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE THROUGH INTERPRETABLE TEXT MINING"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.48009\/2_iis_2026_115","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]],"date-time":"2026-10-06T02:29:02Z","timestamp":1791253742000}},{"DOI":"10.69894\/20101104","title":["Open Text Semantic Network\u2014an exclusive test drive"],"author":[{"given":"Neil","family":"McNaughton","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p xml:lang=\"en\">New cloud-based smart search engine trial leverages www.oilit.com information  resource.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.69894\/20101104","published":{"date-parts":[[2010,12,28]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]],"date-time":"2026-09-03T12:19:20Z","timestamp":1788437960000}},{"DOI":"10.61686\/ipwyn51725","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.61686\/ipwyn51725","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]],"date-time":"2026-09-07T13:00:01Z","timestamp":1788786001000}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.ipm.2026.105176","title":["CORE-DG: Community role-aware contrastive learning on text-attributed dynamic graphs"],"author":[{"given":"Long","family":"Xu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Honghui","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.ipm.2026.105176","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,2]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]],"date-time":"2026-09-25T11:20:59Z","timestamp":1790335259000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0306457326005662?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0306457326005662?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3805696.3846042","title":["Context Selection for Hypothesis and Statistical Evidence Extraction from Full-Text Scientific Articles"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8582-5914","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sai","family":"Koneru","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Pennsylvania State University, University Park, Pennsylvania, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0173-4463","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jian","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Old Dominion University, Norfolk, Virginia, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1464-0848","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sarah","family":"Rajtmajer","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Pennsylvania State University, University Park, Pennsylvania, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3805696.3846042","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T07:02:51Z","timestamp":1791788571000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3805696.3846042","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/conecct68978.2026.11700668","title":["Selective Extraction: A Hallucination-Free Approach to LLM-Based Document Information Extraction"],"author":[{"given":"Vineeth","family":"Rao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"R.V. College of Engineering","place":["Bengaluru, India"],"department":["Department of Computer Science and Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yash","family":"Saraogi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"R.V. College of Engineering","place":["Bengaluru, India"],"department":["Department of Computer Science and Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tulya","family":"Reddy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"R.V. College of Engineering","place":["Bengaluru, India"],"department":["Department of Computer Science and Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mekhala Vinod","family":"Purohit","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"R.V. College of Engineering","place":["Bengaluru, India"],"department":["Department of Computer Science and Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/conecct68978.2026.11700668","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:37:28Z","timestamp":1790624248000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11700512\/11700570\/11700668.pdf?arnumber=11700668","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.iopt.2026.100055","title":["Robust extraction of vehicle trajectories in noisy urban DAS via frequency-decoupled deep learning"],"author":[{"given":"Yuanhang","family":"Zhuo","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jingming","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shanfan","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chaoxian","family":"Qiu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nong","family":"Tian","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xingjie","family":"Lai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jiayao","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaobing","family":"Shi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Peide","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinjun","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jia","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wenjiang","family":"Gao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hongliang","family":"Chang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuhan","family":"Shao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Liyang","family":"Shao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.iopt.2026.100055","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,10]],"date-time":"2026-10-10T22:56:46Z","timestamp":1791673006000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S3051177126000268?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S3051177126000268?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.ins.2026.124202","title":["Decoupled identity learning with prototype alignment for text-image person retrieval"],"author":[{"given":"Jia","family":"Sun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zekai","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yanfeng","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Houjin","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Luyifu","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Minjun","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.ins.2026.124202","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,2]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]],"date-time":"2026-09-23T14:53:24Z","timestamp":1790175204000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0020025526011333?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0020025526011333?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.33889\/ijmems.2026.11.5.088","title":["Deep Learning Based Cosine Text Feature Extraction from Fuzzy Filter Quality Improved Compressed Image Documents"],"author":[{"given":"Sharmila","family":"Chidaravalli","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Global Academy of Technology, VTU, Bangalore, Karnataka, India."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vimuktha E","family":"Salis","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Global Academy of Technology, VTU, Bangalore, Karnataka, India."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>With the surge of process modernization and enhanced data management, the world is advancing towards digitization and automation more than was presumed. The Internet is becoming one of the most favored means of communication and multimedia, coupled with data compression methods, plays a crucial role in controlling the huge volume of data that is being produced on a regular basis. Modeling novel methods for the automatic analysis of compressed data without decompression is pressing requirement of the hour. In this work a deep learning method called Linear Activated Cosine Deep Neural Network based text feature extraction from Fuzzy Filter (LACDNN-FF) quality improved compressed image documents is proposed. The LACDNN-FF method is modeled using an agent AI model. To be more specific, three different agent AIs, namely the Image Enhancement Agent (IEA), Layout Analysis Agent (LAA) and Text Feature Extraction Agent (TFEA) are employed in the design of the LACDNN-FF method. Initially, with the scanned image dataset of compressed image documents as input, two dimensional flexible fuzzy filter-based pre-processing is applied by the IEA with the intent of eliminating noise, thereby generating quality-enhanced compressed documents. Next, Linear Activated Discriminant Analysis with Deep Neural Network-based Segmentation is applied by the LAA to perform document segmentation and classify the contents using pixel-precise semantic segmentation. Finally, Cosine CNN-BiLSTM (Convolutional Neural Network - Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory) neural network based Text Feature Extraction is applied by TFEA for accurate text extraction and text feature processing. The proposed deep learning method LACDNN-FF outperforms existing methods and shows a promising solution for accurate text feature extraction with minimal error rate.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.33889\/ijmems.2026.11.5.088","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T08:44:58Z","timestamp":1790671498000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tmm.2026.3734731","title":["Dual-Balanced Information Bottleneck for Multi-Modal Information Extraction"],"author":[{"given":"Youwei","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhengzhou University","place":["Zhengzhou, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Linpu","family":"Lv","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhengzhou University","place":["Zhengzhou, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Haichuan","family":"Fang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhengzhou University","place":["Zhengzhou, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shizhe","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhengzhou University","place":["Zhengzhou, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tmm.2026.3734731","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T14:22:04Z","timestamp":1790259724000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/6046\/4456689\/11692986.pdf?arnumber=11692986","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Aiming at the challenge of signal detection and feature extraction in complex electromagnetic environment, this paper proposes an adaptive joint optimization method of signal detection and feature. Faced with the problems of traditional methods relying on prior knowledge, insufficient deep learning generalization ability, and poor adaptability to non-stationary noise, a cascaded framework was constructed that includes variable step size LMS (VSS-LMS) interference suppression, multi head self-attention (MHSA) feature extraction, and dynamic threshold decision-making. VSS-LMS achieves fast convergence and steady-state error balance by improving the tongue line function. MHSA uses multi-scale attention mechanism to focus on the characteristics of target signal adaptively. Adaptive threshold dynamically adjusts the threshold based on local noise power estimation. Experiments show that the detection probability of this method is 96% in the signal-to-noise ratio range of -15 dB to 5 dB, which is more than 15% higher than that of the traditional method. The accuracy of feature classification is over 94.3%, and the false alarm probability is stable below 0.01. The three modules cooperate to improve the detection sensitivity and feature discrimination in dynamic electromagnetic environment, and provide a new scheme for intelligent perception of complex electronic information systems.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.54097\/04jw9933","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T00:02:40Z","timestamp":1790812960000},"title":["Research on adaptive signal detection and feature extraction method of complex electronic information system"],"author":[{"given":"Cheng","family":"Yang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Southwest Minzu University, Chengdu, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jceim.org\/index.php\/ojs\/article\/download\/245\/241","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jceim.org\/index.php\/ojs\/article\/download\/245\/241","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.54097\/04jw9933","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Automated bibliographic metadata processing is a key component of intelligent library information systems. However, the use of large language models (LLMs) for bibliographic record generation is associated with factual errors, bibliographic hallucinations, and structurally incorrect metadata. This study proposes a retrieval-constrained workflow in which generative AI operates only on previously extracted and validated information from authoritative bibliographic sources. The workflow integrates deterministic metadata extraction, hierarchical source selection, normalization, multi-stage validation, and specialized AI agents for metadata structuring, Universal Decimal Classification (UDC) assignment, and MARC-formatted record generation. The architecture was evaluated on 200 real ISBN-identified bibliographic records representing 12 languages. The experimental design distinguishes ISBN-only diagnostic conditions, an external Google Books bibliographic metadata baseline, a retrieval-only internal architectural ablation, and the full specialized AI Library workflow. Google Books returned exact ISBN-matched records for 48 of the 200 submitted identifiers (24.0% coverage), resulting in a macro-average manually verified field-level accuracy of 14.8% over the complete dataset. In the controlled GPT-4o comparison, the specialized AI Library workflow increased the macro-average manually verified accuracy of the principal bibliographic fields from 65.0% to 83.2%. It also increased the UDC Recognition Rate from 85.0% to 99.5% and COMARC\/B record-generation coverage from 87.5% to 97.5%, while mean Exact+Partial correspondence with the COBISS reference records increased from 62.1% to 73.6%. Overall, the results demonstrate consistent improvements in bibliographic metadata accuracy, UDC assignment coverage, and COMARC\/B record-level correspondence.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/info17090841","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]],"date-time":"2026-09-01T10:32:18Z","timestamp":1788258738000},"title":["Bibliographic Metadata Extraction Using Deterministic Retrieval and Specialized AI Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5859-9114","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Katia","family":"Rasheva-Yordanova","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Information Sciences, University of Library Studies and Information Technologies, 1784 Sofia, Bulgaria"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yoana","family":"Hadzhiyska","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Information Sciences, University of Library Studies and Information Technologies, 1784 Sofia, Bulgaria"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7220-6206","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ivan","family":"Ivanov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Information Sciences, University of Library Studies and Information Technologies, 1784 Sofia, Bulgaria"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1600-5477","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Iva","family":"Kostadinova","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Information Sciences, University of Library Studies and Information Technologies, 1784 Sofia, Bulgaria"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-9283-7878","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lachezar","family":"Lozev","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Information Sciences, University of Library Studies and Information Technologies, 1784 Sofia, Bulgaria"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anna","family":"Lozeva","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Library Studies and Cultural Heritage, University of Library Studies and Information Technologies, 1784 Sofia, Bulgaria"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2078-2489\/17\/9\/841\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/info17090841","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,30]]}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114\/supp-4","title":["Supplemental Information 4: Structured extraction table for the studies included in the final review, including bibliographic, methodological, and empirical coding fields."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114\/supp-4","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T08:45:22Z","timestamp":1791794722000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4114","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Background\n Laboratory reports vary widely in layout, length, and scan quality, making automated data extraction difficult. While vision-language models (VLMs) handle both visual and textual inputs, it is not fully understood how document-processing granularity affects their extraction accuracy.\nObjective\n We evaluated how model choice, document length, and processing strategy affect VLM performance and efficiency when extracting structured data from multi-page clinical reports.\nMethods\n We designed a controlled experiment using de-identified laboratory reports from Lal PathLabs and Thyrocare. We tested short (1 page), medium (5\u20136 pages), and long (10 pages) documents in both digital and scanned formats. Testing three workflows\u2014Qwen2.5-VL complete-report, Qwen2.5-VL page-wise, and Llama 3.2 Vision page-wise\u2014resulted in 36 experimental runs covering 1,080 expected test occurrences. We compared the extracted records to a manual ground truth, measuring precision, recall, F1-score, overall accuracy, and processing time.\nResults\n Qwen2.5-VL processed page-by-page achieved the highest overall accuracy (98.89%), recall (98.89%), and F1-score (97.80%). This method remained stable even as report length increased, averaging 67.43 seconds per report. Processing the entire report at once with Qwen2.5-VL was much faster (32.08 seconds per report) and highly precise (99.71%). However, its overall accuracy dropped to 94.44% because it missed tests in longer documents. The Llama 3.2 Vision page-wise workflow recorded the lowest accuracy (87.22%) and took the longest (108.12 seconds per report), frequently generating extra, incorrect records.\nConclusions\n The success of VLM data extraction depends heavily on how the input is chunked. Processing reports page-by-page prevents data loss in complex documents, while whole-document processing offers a clear speed advantage. Clinical pipelines should select the strategy that best matches their specific speed and accuracy requirements.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11152842\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]],"date-time":"2026-09-25T07:16:54Z","timestamp":1790320614000},"title":["Evaluating Vision-Language Models for Structured Information Extraction from Heterogeneous Multi-Page Laboratory Reports"],"author":[{"given":"Ashish","family":"Katyal","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Birla Institute of Technology and Science, Pilani"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ashwani","family":"Bhatnagar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Indian Institute of Technology Jammu"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11152842\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11152842\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11152842\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/gtss68743.2026.11679716","title":["Healing with Words: A Comparative Analysis of Learning Models for Sentiment Extraction in Healthcare Text"],"author":[{"given":"Gaurav Kr.","family":"Rajput","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Teerthanker Mahaveer University","place":["Moradabad, U.P., India"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Namit","family":"Gupta","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing Science Engineering & Technology, Bennett University","place":["Greater Noida, U.P., India"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Saurabh Kumar","family":"Srivastava","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Teerthanker Mahaveer University","place":["Moradabad, U.P., India"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/gtss68743.2026.11679716","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,2]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T19:09:33Z","timestamp":1789585773000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11679571\/11679530\/11679716.pdf?arnumber=11679716","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.4208\/jics.v1.n2.p101","title":["Retrieval of Sports Video Clips Using Audio-Visual Features and Text Information"],"author":[{"given":"Yaqin","family":"Zhao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Automation, Nanjing University of Science & Technology, Nanjing 210094"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xin","family":"He","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Automation, Nanjing University of Science & Technology, Nanjing 210094"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xianzhong","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Management and Engineering, Nanjing University, Nanjing 210093"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Video \u00a0clip \u00a0retrieval \u00a0plays \u00a0a \u00a0critical \u00a0role \u00a0in \u00a0the \u00a0content-based \u00a0sports \u00a0video \u00a0retrieval. \u00a0This \u00a0paper \nproposes a content-based retrieval strategy of sports video clip in which visual and auditory features and text \ninformation are extracted to locate similar video clips. Because in sports game play scenes are concerned and \ninterested \u00a0for \u00a0most \u00a0audiences, \u00a0a \u00a0long \u00a0sport \u00a0video \u00a0is \u00a0first \u00a0automatically \u00a0segmented \u00a0into \u00a0play \u00a0segments \u00a0and \ntime-out \u00a0segments \u00a0based \u00a0on \u00a0audio \u00a0feature \u00a0in \u00a0compressed \u00a0domain. \u00a0And \u00a0then \u00a0we \u00a0obtain \u00a0similar \u00a0video \u00a0clips \nbased \u00a0on \u00a0sliding \u00a0shot \u00a0window \u00a0algorithm \u00a0and \u00a0equivalence \u00a0relation \u00a0theory. \u00a0Finally \u00a0similar \u00a0video \u00a0clips \u00a0are \nranked \u00a0by \u00a0visual \u00a0factor \u00a0and \u00a0order \u00a0factor \u00a0in \u00a0terms \u00a0of \u00a0longest \u00a0common \u00a0sequence \u00a0(LCS). \u00a0The \u00a0experimental \nresults \u00a0showed \u00a0that \u00a0the \u00a0proposed \u00a0method \u00a0could \u00a0effectively \u00a0and \u00a0efficiently \u00a0retrieve \u00a0similar \u00a0video \u00a0clips \ncorresponding with a query clip in a sports video of TV program.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4208\/jics.v1.n2.p101","published":{"date-parts":[[2006]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T10:35:44Z","timestamp":1790678144000}},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-032-39708-9_31","title":["AI-Driven Error Mitigation in Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) Devices for Physical Simulations"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-9989-2209","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Weihao","family":"Kong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-032-39708-9_31","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,22]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,21]],"date-time":"2026-09-21T06:24:31Z","timestamp":1789971871000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/978-3-032-39708-9_31","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.im.2026.104439","title":["Embedding text in crowdfunding campaign cover images: The impact on funding outcomes"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9156-0051","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dongyu","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5615-9967","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jennifer Jie","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fujun","family":"Lai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qiuxian","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.im.2026.104439","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,15]],"date-time":"2026-09-15T06:17:04Z","timestamp":1789453024000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0378720626001394?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0378720626001394?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tit.2026.3738454","title":["The strong converse exponent of composable randomness extraction against quantum side information"],"author":[{"given":"Roberto","family":"Rubboli","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Copenhagen, Universitetsparken 5","department":["Department of Mathematical Sciences"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5410-3329","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Marco","family":"Tomamichel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore","department":["Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tit.2026.3738454","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T19:02:38Z","timestamp":1790708558000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/18\/4667673\/11715835.pdf?arnumber=11715835","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.4208\/jics.v2.n3.p215","title":["Exploiting word positional information in ngram model for Chinese text input method"],"author":[{"given":"Jinghui","family":"Xiao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Techniques, Harbin Institute of Technology, Harbin, 150001, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Bingquan","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Techniques, Harbin Institute of Technology, Harbin, 150001, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaolong","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Techniques, Harbin Institute of Technology, Harbin, 150001, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>This  paper  aims  to  improve  the  performance  of  the  Pinyin-to-Character  Conversion  system \nwhich  is  the  core  of  Chinese  text  input  method.  The  ngram  model  is  the  current  solution  to  the  Pinyin-to-\nCharacter  Conversion  system.  This  paper  enhances  the  traditional  ngram  model  by  relaxing  its  stationary \nhypothesis and exploiting the word positional information. The Non-stationary ngram (NS ngram) model is \nproposed. Several related issues are discussed in detail, including the formal definition, the model implement, \nthe  training  algorithm  and  the  space  complexity  of  the  NS  ngram  model.  Evaluated  on  the  Pinyin-to-\nCharacter Conversion task, the NS ngram model outperforms the traditional ngram model significantly with \ngreat  error  rate  reductions.  Meanwhile,  the  training  algorithm  presented  in  this  paper  can  estimate  the \nparameters in the NS ngram model effectively and efficiently.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4208\/jics.v2.n3.p215","published":{"date-parts":[[2007]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T10:36:44Z","timestamp":1790678204000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Educational institutions and professionals must analyze student performance to identify ways to improve each student's academic achievement. Predicting how well students will do in universities and colleges is crucial for managing their time effectively. Predicting students' academic achievement has been the subject of some relevant research activity since the advent of deep learning. Students' academic performance is affected by various factors, yet there is a general trend of similar intelligence and learning styles among students at the same university. The goal of this research is to use deep learning algorithm and historical data to forecast how well students will do in academics. Academic performance analysis can be enhanced by combining a Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer (T5) with a Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) model. This allows us to leverage the capabilities of both architectures. In contrast to Bi-LSTM's adeptness at capturing sequential dependencies, T5's text-to-text method offers a versatile framework for encoding and processing a wide range of academic data. Accuracy in activities such as requirements classification and short response grading can be enhanced using this hybrid technique. The proposed T5-BiLSTM model is used to accurately identify the predictive variables from academic datasets that majorly influence the academic performance. This research is helpful for educational institutions to identify the predictive variables that are used to enhance the student academic progress.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.19139\/soic-2310-5070-3456","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,17]],"date-time":"2026-10-17T04:29:37Z","timestamp":1792211377000},"title":["Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory Integrated Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer to Identify Predictive Variables for Students\u2019 Academic Performance Analysis"],"author":[{"given":"Neha","family":"Kandula","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"family":"Anil Sharma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ram","family":"Kumar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/iapress.org\/index.php\/soic\/article\/download\/3456\/2167","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/iapress.org\/index.php\/soic\/article\/download\/3456\/2167","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.19139\/soic-2310-5070-3456","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,26]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>The extraction of treatment response information from unstructured clinical notes is a critical task in oncology research and clinical practice, particularly for lung cancer, where therapeutic paradigms evolve rapidly. Despite the promising capabilities of advanced natural language processing models, their opaque nature significantly limits clinical adoption due to a lack of interpretability and trustworthiness. This paper investigates the development and implementation of an explainable clinical text mining framework explicitly designed to extract treatment response trajectories from unstructured electronic health records pertaining to lung cancer cohorts. By integrating transformer-based language models with robust explainability techniques, the proposed methodology aims to bridge the gap between high predictive performance and clinical transparency. The analysis encompasses an exhaustive review of current natural language processing applications in oncology, a detailed explication of the architectural design prioritizing attention-based interpretability, and a rigorous evaluation against traditional black-box methodologies. The findings demonstrate that augmenting extraction models with explanatory mechanisms not only maintains state-of-the-art accuracy but also significantly enhances clinician trust by providing verifiable linguistic evidence for every automated extraction. This research underscores the necessity of transparent artificial intelligence in high-stakes medical domains and provides a scalable blueprint for deploying explainable text mining systems in real-world oncology workflows.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.66238\/jaiec57","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,2]],"date-time":"2026-10-02T04:47:33Z","timestamp":1790916453000},"title":["Explainable Clinical Text Mining for Treatment Response Extraction in Lung Cancer Notes"],"author":[{"family":"Aoi Ito","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"family":"Bernard Darren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jaiec.org\/index.php\/JAIEC\/article\/download\/57\/66","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jaiec.org\/index.php\/JAIEC\/article\/download\/57\/66","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.66238\/jaiec57","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,15]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Online media coverage may transmit information across supply-chain relationships, yet converting such signals into evidence requires a clear separation between text measurement, network alignment, and statistical inference. This study examines whether focal-firm media attention is associated with connected partners\u2019 annual-report digital disclosure and develops a manufacturing-oriented workflow for directed supply-chain analysis. We construct a firm\u2013partner\u2013year panel for Chinese non-financial A-share firms from 2007 to 2024 by combining aggregated CFND media indicators, annual-report digital-term frequencies, CNRDS supplier\u2013customer links, and CSMAR controls. Fixed-effects models estimate conditional associations on directed edges. The baseline specification shows a positive association between focal-firm coverage and partner digital disclosure (\u03b2 = 0.048, SE = 0.020), while alternative aggregate coverage measures yield coefficients with the same sign; mechanism and subgroup results remain observational and descriptive. These findings do not establish causality or realized interfirm digital collaboration because annual-report word frequency is an imperfect disclosure proxy. A Chinese- BERT\u2013MLP event-and-tone module is therefore retained only as a future extension pending article-level text, human- reviewed labels, and out-of-sample evaluation.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.4108\/eetsis.14249","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T13:30:10Z","timestamp":1791811810000},"title":["Text Mining and Machine Learning for New-MediaPublic-Opinion Spillovers in Manufacturing Supply-Chain Digital Collaboration"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-3020-6002","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Chengyan","family":"Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/057hdmq26","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Tianjin Foreign Studies University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/publications.eai.eu\/index.php\/sis\/article\/download\/14249\/4450","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/publications.eai.eu\/index.php\/sis\/article\/download\/14249\/4450","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4108\/eetsis.14249","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7390638","title":["From Crash Narratives to Structured Liability and Severity: An LLM Information-Extraction Pipeline for Autonomous-Vehicle Insurance Triage"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-0716-524X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Doyeon","family":"Jang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>As autonomous-vehicle (AV) fleets scale, insurers must convert a growing volume of unstructured collision reports into the structured signals that reserving and subrogation require. Two regulatory regimes already mandate free-text crash narratives-the U.S. NHTSA Standing General Order (SGO 2021-01) and the California DMV's OL 316 collision reports-yet these narratives are largely unexploited for predictive use. We build and evaluate a schemaconstrained large-language-model (LLM) pipeline that codes these narratives into a structured representation of collision type, pre-crash maneuver, engagement state, operating-environment conditions, and a narrative-described contributory party. We evaluate it primarily against a distant-supervision key: the structured codes the reporting entities themselves file alongside each narrative, which supply 31,204 supervised field decisions across ten fields. The best model attains a median \u03ba of 0.703 against this key, with the residual disagreement dominated by conditions the narratives simply do not state rather than by mis-extraction. We then ask whether narrative text adds predictive value for injury severity beyond the structured fields insurers already collect, using entity-grouped cross-validation and a leakage-controlled design that excludes any text-derived restatement of the outcome. Adding text yields a quadratic-weighted-\u03ba lift of 0.180 over the structured-only baseline.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7390638","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,2]],"date-time":"2026-09-02T18:24:50Z","timestamp":1788373490000}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7483265","title":["Affordances Without a Body: How Text-Only Large Language Models Represent and Use Affordance-Related Information"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-5637-4783","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Giorgia","family":"Coscia","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9701-1632","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Angelo","family":"Gaeta","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6899-4396","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Francesco","family":"Orciuoli","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Text-only Large Language Models (LLMs) lack direct sensorimotor experience, yet they are trained on language produced by embodied agents. This raises the embodiment problem of whether language can transmit enough regularities about body\u2013world interaction for a disembodied model to reconstruct action-related information. Affordances provide a particularly stringent test case, since by definition they concern action possibilities that depend on the current state of an object or environment. Rather than asking whether LLMs simply \u201dpossess\u201d affordances in a Gibsonian sense, we investigate how text-only LLMs rep-resent and use affordance-related information. We introduce an experimental framework that distinguishes three levels of affordance-related processing (latent representational accessibility, context-sensitive behavioral use, and their association), together with a fourth analysis probing generalization to a finer affordance distinction. Applying it to three architectures (GPT-OSS-20B, Qwen3.5-27B, and Gemma-4-31B-it) through probing, geometric, and behavioral analyses, we find that affordance information is consistently linearly decodable and organized along coherent latent directions, though the categories do not form fully separated clusters. In some architectures, stronger latent affordance representations are further associated with greater contextual behavioral sensitivity. The overall pattern is graded and architecture-dependent rather than all-or-none, suggesting that linguistic experience can reconstruct some action-related regularities without providing evidence of genuine sensorimotor grounding or embodied cognition.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7483265","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,18]],"date-time":"2026-09-18T13:59:40Z","timestamp":1789739980000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>The integration of natural language understanding into conversational commerce requires robustly extracting structured intents from idiomatic text. While cloud-based models are highly capable, their latency, privacy risks, and operational costs necessitate efficient local alternatives. To address this, we empirically evaluate locally deployed, open-weights Large Language Models (LLMs) on parsing multi-intent shopping cart commands into deterministic JSON schemas. We benchmark multiple models across minimal, extended, and few-shot prompting paradigms, utilizing a dual-axis framework to simultaneously measure extraction quality (operation F1-score, exact match, schema validity) and hardware performance (latency, GPU power consumption). Our evaluation highlights a fundamental trade-off between schema compliance and computational overhead, demonstrating how advanced prompting techniques improve parsing accuracy while measurably impacting inference latency and power draw. Ultimately, this comprehensive profiling identifies optimal model configurations for resource-constrained environments: a well-chosen 3-8B parameter model (specifically Granite4 3B) paired with few-shot prompting recovers over 95% of the quality of the largest evaluated model at a coarse indicative estimate of 40\u00d7 lower operational energy per command, establishing a practical baseline for deploying cost-effective LLMs as efficiency candidates in resource-constrained environments.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/info17101020","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,14]],"date-time":"2026-10-14T13:02:40Z","timestamp":1791982960000},"title":["Benchmarking Local Large Language Models for Structured Knowledge Extraction in Conversational Commerce"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-4210-5756","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ratomir","family":"Karlovi\u0107","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Informatics, Juraj Dobrila University of Pula, Zagreba\u010dka 30, 52100 Pula, Croatia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-1965-7822","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Luka","family":"Sever","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Informatics, Juraj Dobrila University of Pula, Zagreba\u010dka 30, 52100 Pula, Croatia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-9740-7381","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Marijela","family":"Mili\u010devi\u0107","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Informatics, Juraj Dobrila University of Pula, Zagreba\u010dka 30, 52100 Pula, Croatia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3015-1024","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sandi Baressi","family":"\u0160egota","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Informatics, Juraj Dobrila University of Pula, Zagreba\u010dka 30, 52100 Pula, Croatia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0323-2600","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Vedran","family":"Mrzljak","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Engineering Rijeka, University of Rijeka, Vukovarska 58, 51000 Rijeka, Croatia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5964-245X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ivan","family":"Lorencin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Informatics, Juraj Dobrila University of Pula, Zagreba\u010dka 30, 52100 Pula, Croatia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2078-2489\/17\/10\/1020\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/info17101020","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,14]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Text extraction from an image is characterized by various challenges that result in various evolving solutions at current times. Existing solutions using artificial intelligence (AI) do have some promising outcomes, but also have their own shortcomings associated with accuracy and efficiency. The manuscript introduces a simplified and yet innovative AI framework towards extracting text by unifying character region-aware text detection. The model uses a convolutional neural network (CNN) along with transformer-centric optical character recognition (OCR) towards better performance on scene text recognition. Analysis is carried out on the standard and benchmarked International Conference on Document Analysis and Recognition 2015 (ICDAR 2015) dataset to find that the proposed model accomplished 94.7% accuracy and 0.82 seconds per image of response time, which are much significant improvements in performance, while observed in contrast to existing AI models, proving the model to deliver high-performance and cost-effective outcomes.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.11591\/ijai.v15.i5.pp4724-4732","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,11]],"date-time":"2026-10-11T06:53:30Z","timestamp":1791701610000},"title":["A deep learning framework for robust text extraction from heterogeneous image sources"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-8823-0528","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dayananda","family":"Kodala Jayaram","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5547-0761","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Puttegowda","family":"Devegowda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/ijai.iaescore.com\/index.php\/IJAI\/article\/viewFile\/29671\/15436","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/ijai.iaescore.com\/index.php\/IJAI\/article\/viewFile\/29671\/15436","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.11591\/ijai.v15.i5.pp4724-4732","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3833541.3833546","title":["A Text Classification Method for Telecom Fraud Based on Fusion of TF-IDF Feature Extraction and BERT Model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-3077-6996","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yihui","family":"Gan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Harbin Institute of Technology, Harbin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3833541.3833546","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,24]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,15]],"date-time":"2026-09-15T03:24:32Z","timestamp":1789442672000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3833541.3833546","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/taslpro.2026.3739130","title":["Inter-Speaker Relative Cues for Two-Stage Text-Guided Target Speech Extraction"],"author":[{"given":"Wang","family":"Dai","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Tampere University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Archontis","family":"Politis","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tampere University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tuomas","family":"Virtanen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tampere University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/taslpro.2026.3739130","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]],"date-time":"2026-09-30T19:21:34Z","timestamp":1790796094000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/10723155\/10818373\/11716739.pdf?arnumber=11716739","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/acit69831.2026.11700021","title":["A Modular Information Extraction and LLM-Based Automated Reporting Framework for Multilingual Disaster Analysis"],"author":[{"given":"Zeynep","family":"Karaca","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"F\u0131rat University","place":["Elazig, Turkey"],"department":["Department of Computer Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"\u0130lhan","family":"Ayd\u0131n","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"F\u0131rat University","place":["Elazig, Turkey"],"department":["Department of Computer Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nurhayat","family":"Dere","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"F\u0131rat University","place":["Elazig, Turkey"],"department":["Department of Computer Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/acit69831.2026.11700021","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:25:36Z","timestamp":1790623536000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11699778\/11699878\/11700021.pdf?arnumber=11700021","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/acit69831.2026.11700212","title":["Aggregating Duplicate Edges in Text-Derived Knowledge Graphs"],"author":[{"given":"Illia","family":"Dagil","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Taras Shevchenko National University of Kiev","place":["Kyiv, Ukraine"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Iryna","family":"Vergunova","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Taras Shevchenko National University of Kiev","place":["Kyiv, Ukraine"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/acit69831.2026.11700212","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:15:08Z","timestamp":1790622908000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11699778\/11699878\/11700212.pdf?arnumber=11700212","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.inffus.2026.104846","title":["Text-augmented patch\u2013scene collaborative network for joint hyperspectral and LiDAR classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3639-0456","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Li","family":"Lv","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-3663-0554","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhenyang","family":"Xie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8404-2464","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hongmin","family":"Gao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6802-9083","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shufang","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Haihua","family":"Xie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wenping","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shuiping","family":"Kang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.inffus.2026.104846","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,3]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T21:14:51Z","timestamp":1791926091000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S1566253526007220?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S1566253526007220?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>This study evaluated the performance of Turnitin, a tool widely used across colleges and universities for detecting AI-generated text. Our sample of human-written text comprised 100 introduction sections from psychology journal articles published in 2016. Next, we generated new versions of the texts using ChatGPT (GPT-4o and GPT-5). Using Turnitin\u2019s AI score for each piece of writing, we found a false positive rate (incorrectly classifying human-written text as AI-generated) of only 1\u202f%. In contrast, Turnitin\u2019s false negative rate (incorrectly classifying AI-generated text as human-written) was substantially higher and depended on the model used to generate the text. Specifically, the latest version of ChatGPT produced text more likely to be misclassified as human-written than the previous version. These findings highlight important limitations in the reliability of automated AI-detection tools, particularly with respect to false negative rates and model-dependent performance. As such, AI-detection scores should be interpreted cautiously, especially when used to infer the presence or absence of AI-generated text in academic writing.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1515\/opis-2025-0056","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]],"date-time":"2026-09-08T10:00:33Z","timestamp":1788861633000},"title":["Distinguishing Between AI-Generated and Human-Written Academic Text Using Turnitin"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8339-8832","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Robin S.S.","family":"Kramer","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Psychology, Sport Science & Wellbeing, University of Lincoln , Lincoln , UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-7797-3738","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nat\u00e1lia","family":"Javorkov\u00e1","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Psychology , Palack\u00fd University Olomouc , Olomouc , Czech Republic"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.degruyterbrill.com\/document\/doi\/10.1515\/opis-2025-0056\/xml","content-type":"application\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.degruyterbrill.com\/document\/doi\/10.1515\/opis-2025-0056\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/opis-2025-0056","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,1]]}},{"DOI":"10.1504\/ijict.2026.10081429","title":["An NLP-based large-scale information extraction framework for modelling responsibility relations in judicial web documents"],"author":[{"given":"Shuai","family":"Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1504\/ijict.2026.10081429","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T13:00:51Z","timestamp":1790859651000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/www.inderscienceonline.com\/doi\/full\/10.1504\/IJICT.2026.10081429","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1080\/10095020.2026.2732425","title":["SPNet: segmentation-to-poly network for building footprint extraction"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8804-6390","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Huaigang","family":"Huang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Shenzhen University","place":["Shenzhen, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0745-5158","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ruisheng","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Shenzhen University","place":["Shenzhen, China"]},{"name":"Shenzhen University","place":["Shenzhen, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-7644-1755","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jinpeng","family":"Lei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Shenzhen University","place":["Shenzhen, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1726-4697","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kun","family":"Zhou","sequence":"